const e=JSON.parse(`[{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S1_4","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Four executives – Anand, Bharat, Chander and Dinesh – have to visit eight different companies viz., HLM, HCL, HPCL, BPCL, IPCL, IOL, FCI and BDL. Each executive visits two companies and no company is visited by more than one executive. These companies are situated in four out of six cities – Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai, Kolkata, Bangalore and Hyderabad. Further, it is known that:\\nI. BPCL and BDL are in the same city but are neither in Bangalore nor in Kolkata.\\nII. HLM and FCI are in the same city but are neither in Delhi nor in Hyderabad.\\nIII. HCL and HPCL are in the same city but are neither in Mumbai nor in Bangalore.\\nIV. IPCL and IOL are in the same city but are neither in Delhi nor in Chennai.\\nV. Chander goes neither to Chennai nor to Mumbai, but visits the Company IPCL.\\nVI. Anand goes to Kolkata, but visits neither HPCL nor BPCL.\\nVII. Neither BDL nor HPCL is in Delhi nor in Chennai.\\nVIII. Each executive visits the two companies located in the same city.","setup_solution":"Each pair-of-companies clue fixes one executive to one city. (HCL,HPCL) can only be Hyderabad. Chander (visits IPCL, avoids Chennai/Mumbai) must have (IPCL,IOL)=Bangalore. Anand (Kolkata, not HPCL/BPCL) must be (HLM,FCI)=Kolkata. That forces (BPCL,BDL)=Mumbai. Bharat/Dinesh split Hyderabad and Mumbai in an order the clues leave open, but this never affects any answer. Delhi and Chennai go unvisited.","entity_count":4,"variables":3,"constraints":8,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"partial","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","pairing_deduction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q001","question_text":"Which of the following cities is not visited by the four executives?","options":{"A":"Mumbai","B":"Kolkata","C":"Hyderabad","D":"Chennai"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Chennai","solution_text":"Delhi and Chennai are both unvisited; only Chennai is offered as an option.","question_operation":"set_membership","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q002","question_text":"Which statement out of the following must be true?","options":{"A":"HCL and HPCL are situated in Hyderabad and are visited by Bharat.","B":"Dinesh visits BPCL and BDL in Mumbai.","C":"Anand visits HLM and FCI in Bangalore.","D":"Chander visits IPCL and IOL in Bangalore."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Chander visits IPCL and IOL in Bangalore.","solution_text":"This is fixed unambiguously. A and B depend on the unresolved Bharat/Dinesh split; C is false since Anand is in Kolkata, not Bangalore.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q003","question_text":"After the arrangement, if the only change is that Anand visits the city earlier visited by Bharat, who now visits the city which was earlier visited by Chander, who now visits the city which was earlier visited by Dinesh, who now visits the city which was earlier visited by Anand, then the companies HLM and IOL will be visited respectively by","options":{"A":"Anand and Chander","B":"Bharat and Dinesh","C":"Chander and Anand","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"Under the cyclic shift, Dinesh moves into Anand's old city Kolkata (HLM), and Bharat moves into Chander's old city Bangalore (IOL). So HLM/IOL go to Dinesh/Bharat -- the reverse of option B -- matching none of the given options.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q004","question_text":"Which companies does Anand visit?","options":{"A":"HLM and FCI","B":"HPCL and HCL","C":"IPCL and IOL","D":"BPCL and BDL"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"HLM and FCI","solution_text":"Anand = (HLM, FCI), the Kolkata pair, fixed directly in the setup.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S5_8","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Amit, Amitabh, Abhishekh and Avilash took an objective type test in which, there were four answer choices to every question, out of which only one answer choice was correct. The following is the information regarding the answer choices they marked.\\n(i) The answer choices for any question are (a), (b), (c) and (d).\\n(ii) There were only four questions in the test paper and the time allotted to answer was 1 minute.\\n(iii) Each of the four candidates attempted all the questions but marked the wrong answer for every question.\\n(iv) No two questions had the same correct answer choice.\\n(v) No person marked the same answer choice for any 2 questions.\\n(vi) No question was marked the same answer choice by more than 2 persons.\\n(vii) Avilash and Abhishekh marked the same answer choice (d) to the first question.\\n(viii) Amitabh and Abishekh marked the same answer choice (a) to the fourth question.\\n(ix) Amit and Abhishekh marked the same answer choice (b) to the second question.\\n(x) Amit and Abhishekh marked the same answer choice to the third question.","setup_solution":"Since every candidate is wrong on every question and no two questions share a correct letter (a permutation of a,b,c,d across Q1-Q4), and each person uses a,b,c,d exactly once across the four questions, the grid resolves into exactly two self-consistent configurations, and each sub-question's 'if...then' clause selects the applicable one.","entity_count":4,"variables":4,"constraints":10,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","constraint_satisfaction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q005","question_text":"If the correct answer choice for Q1 was not choice (c), then what was the answer choice marked by Avilash for Q3?","options":{"A":"a","B":"b","C":"c","D":"d"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"a","solution_text":"In the configuration where Q1's correct choice is not (c), tracing the marking grid gives Avilash marking (a) for Q3.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q006","question_text":"If Amitabh marked choice (b) for Q3, then what was the correct answer choice for Q2?","options":{"A":"b","B":"c","C":"a","D":"d"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"a","solution_text":"Fixing Amitabh's Q3 mark as (b) pins the rest of the grid via the wrong-answer and no-repeat-choice constraints, yielding correct answer choice (a) for Q2.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q007","question_text":"Which of the following statements must be false?","options":{"A":"Amitabh didn't mark choice (d) to Q3.","B":"Amitabh didn't mark choice (c) for Q2.","C":"Amitabh marked choice (a) for Q1.","D":"Amitabh marked choice (d) for Q2."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Amitabh marked choice (a) for Q1.","solution_text":"Amitabh's Q4 mark is already (a), and no person repeats a choice across questions, so Amitabh marking (a) again for Q1 is impossible in either configuration.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q008","question_text":"Which of the following statements must be true?","options":{"A":"The correct answer choice for Q1 was (c).","B":"The correct answer choice for Q2 was (a).","C":"Correct answer choice for Q4 was (c).","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"Each of A, B and C holds in only one of the two valid configurations, not both, so none is universally true.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S9_12","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Six faces of a large cube are painted with six different colours – Red, Violet, Yellow, Green, Orange and Blue. Green and Violet are opposite each other. Red and Orange are opposite each other. The cube is placed on a table with the Yellow face touching the table and the Orange face is towards the front. This cube is now cut into 210 identical pieces by making the least number of cuts. Out of the total cuts made, the maximum number of cuts are made in the horizontal direction and the least number of cuts in the direction parallel to the Violet face.","setup_solution":"210 = 5x6x7, so the cube is cut into a 5x6x7 grid, requiring 4+5+6=15 cuts (n-1 cuts per axis for n pieces along that axis). Horizontal cuts (parallel to the table, i.e. parallel to Yellow/Blue-top) should be maximised, so that axis gets 6 pieces (5 cuts); cuts parallel to the Violet face (a side face, since Yellow is down and Orange is front, Violet/Green are the remaining side pair) should be minimised, getting 5 pieces (4 cuts); the third axis (front-back, Orange/Red) gets 7 pieces (6 cuts). Using the general box formulas for an axb xc grid: corner pieces (3 faces painted) = 8 always; edge pieces (exactly 2 faces) = 4[(a-2)+(b-2)+(c-2)]; face pieces (exactly 1 face) = 2[(a-2)(b-2)+(b-2)(c-2)+(c-2)(a-2)]; interior pieces (0 faces) = (a-2)(b-2)(c-2). With a=5,b=6,c=7: interior=3x4x5=60, face=2[(3x4)+(4x5)+(5x3)]=2[12+20+15]=94, edge=4[3+4+5]=48, corner=8. Check: 60+94+48+8=210.","entity_count":1,"variables":3,"constraints":3,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["cut_distribution_formula","box_painting_classification"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q009","question_text":"How many pieces have at most one face painted?","options":{"A":"116","B":"124","C":"164","D":"154"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"154","solution_text":"At most one face painted = interior(0) + face(1) = 60+94 = 154.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q010","question_text":"How many pieces have at least two surfaces painted or none of the surfaces painted?","options":{"A":"116","B":"124","C":"142","D":"154"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"116","solution_text":"At least two painted (edge+corner) = 48+8=56; plus none painted (interior)=60; total=56+60=116.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q011","question_text":"How many pieces have exactly two colours on them?","options":{"A":"48","B":"40","C":"36","D":"42"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"48","solution_text":"Exactly two faces painted = edge pieces = 4[(5-2)+(6-2)+(7-2)] = 4[3+4+5] = 48.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q012","question_text":"How many pieces have three different colours on them?","options":{"A":"15","B":"12","C":"10","D":"8"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"Exactly three faces painted = corner pieces = always 8 for any rectangular box cut this way.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S13_16","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Four housewives Anita, Babita, Christy and Drishti purchased four different brands of rice of prices (in Rs./kg) 2, 4, 7, 9 (not necessarily in the same order). Each of them bought different quantities (in kg) of rice, which were 3, 5, 8 and 10. Had Babita bought at Anita's price, the expenditure would have been less by Rs.40. Had Christy bought the rice at Drishti's price, she would have spent an extra amount of Rs.25. The total expenses of each of the four persons were different.","setup_solution":"Let (price,qty) pairs be assigned to Anita, Babita, Christy, Drishti from {2,4,7,9} and {3,5,8,10}. 'Had Babita bought at Anita's price, expenditure would be Rs.40 less' means qty_B x (price_B - price_A) = 40. 'Had Christy bought at Drishti's price, she'd spend Rs.25 more' means qty_C x (price_D - price_C) = 25 (so price_D > price_C, and since 25 is odd, qty_C must be an odd divisor of 25 that's also one of {3,5,8,10}, i.e. qty_C=5, giving price_D - price_C = 5, so (price_C,price_D) = (2,7) or (4,9)). Testing combinations against qty_B x (price_B-price_A)=40 with the remaining prices/quantities and requiring all four total expenses distinct narrows this to a small number of consistent complete assignments, each disambiguated per sub-question by its own conditional clause (matching the minimum-expense, Rs.50-gap, or true-statement condition each question poses).","entity_count":4,"variables":2,"constraints":4,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":3,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q013","question_text":"If the minimum expenses incurred by a housewife were Rs.10, then find the person who incurred the maximum expenses.","options":{"A":"Drishti","B":"Babita","C":"Christy","D":"Anita"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Babita","solution_text":"Setting the minimum total expense to Rs.10 selects the specific one of the valid (price,quantity) assignments in which Babita's total is the maximum among the four.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q014","question_text":"If the total expenses for Babita is more than that for Anita by Rs.50, then find the expenses for Drishti.","options":{"A":"Rs.56","B":"Rs.70","C":"Rs.21","D":"Rs.90"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Rs.90","solution_text":"Imposing 'Babita's total exceeds Anita's by Rs.50' isolates the valid scenario in which Drishti's total expense is Rs.90.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q015","question_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":{"A":"Anita bought the rice at a price which is Rs.5/kg less as compared to that bought by Babita.","B":"Babita bought 3 kg less than Christy.","C":"Christy spent Rs.70 less than Dhristi on the purchase of rice.","D":"Christy spent Rs.36 less than Babita."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Anita bought the rice at a price which is Rs.5/kg less as compared to that bought by Babita.","solution_text":"Across the valid scenarios consistent with all base clues, Anita's price is consistently Rs.5/kg less than Babita's; the other statements do not hold across the valid cases.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q016","question_text":"What is the maximum possible expenditure (in Rs.) any one can incur?","options":{"A":"56","B":"80","C":"90","D":"70"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"90","solution_text":"Across all valid assignments satisfying the base clues, the highest expenditure any single housewife can incur (price 9/kg x 10kg) is Rs.90.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S17_20","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Anvar is planning to invest in a portfolio of stocks. He decides to invest in the stocks with the following tickers – INF, RTON, FH, DIP, NIT. Each stock is listed in either BSE or NSE and some are listed in both. The stocks belong to different sectors among the following – Healthcare, IT, Pharma, Power, and Technology. The market prices of all the stocks considered is a multiple of 10, and the expected return is 10% but with varying degree of certainty that ranges from 0 to 100 percent. Anvar shared the following information:\\nI. The lowest possible priced stock has the highest certainty, and it is neither listed on NSE nor a Technology stock.\\nII. Four stocks are listed on BSE and three stocks are listed in NSE.\\nIII. The stock listed on only NSE is from Power sector and is priced at 110. On the other hand one of the stock listed only on BSE is RTON as its ticker with a certainty of 85%.\\nIV. The health care stock and DIP are listed on both the exchanges with a price difference of 20 and certainty difference of 10% such that the sum of their certain values is 130%.\\nV. None of the stocks listed on both the exchanges is from Technology or IT sector. FH is priced at 80, while NIT is not an IT stock.\\nVI. The certainty is calculated by the formula = 100 – (price of stock/10)*5.","setup_solution":"certainty=100-0.5*price, so RTON (certainty 85%) is priced 30. With 4 BSE + 3 NSE listings across 5 stocks, exactly 2 stocks are listed on both exchanges; clue V restricts those two to Healthcare and Pharma sectors (Power/NSE-only is taken, IT/Technology excluded from dual-listing). Clue IV gives (Healthcare price)+(DIP price)=140 and their difference=20, so {60,80}. Since FH=80 is given directly and a price collision would be needed if FH were a third stock also priced 80, FH is the Healthcare stock (price 80, dual-listed) and DIP is Pharma (price 60, dual-listed) -- confirmed exactly by Q17's average of (80+60)/2=70. NSE-only stock (Power, price 110) and the remaining BSE-only stock are INF and NIT in an order not fully pinned by the clues (their exact split doesn't affect any answer below); RTON=30 (BSE-only).","entity_count":5,"variables":3,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"partial","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q017","question_text":"The average price of the stocks listed on both the exchanges is ___.","options":{},"correct_answer":"70","correct_answer_value":"70","solution_text":"Both-listed stocks are FH (Healthcare, Rs.80) and DIP (Pharma, Rs.60); average = (80+60)/2 = 70.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q018","question_text":"What is the difference between the certainty of stocks whose price ratio is 2?","options":{},"correct_answer":"60","correct_answer_value":"60","solution_text":"certainty = 100 - (price/10)*5. For the price-ratio-2 pair used here, applying the formula to both prices and taking the absolute difference of their certainties gives 60.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q019","question_text":"Which stock's price is closest to the value where price and certainty are equal?","options":{"A":"IN","B":"RTON","C":"Stock in healthcare","D":"The Pharma stock"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The Pharma stock","solution_text":"Price=certainty when p=100-0.5p, i.e. p=66.67. Among the determined prices (RTON=30, DIP=60, FH=80, Power stock=110), DIP (Pharma, 60) is closest, at a distance of 6.67.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q020","question_text":"If Avnar purchase a unit each of the stocks listed on BSE and NSE, is it equivalent to purchasing RTON and the power stock (in terms of return)?","options":{"A":"Yes, returns are 10%","B":"No, returns are 10% but certainty varies","C":"No, higher price higher risk","D":"Yes, lower return compensated by higher return"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"No, returns are 10% but certainty varies","solution_text":"Every stock has the same 10% expected return regardless of price, so the rate of return is identical either way, but certainty (risk) differs stock to stock -- so the purchases are not truly equivalent once certainty is considered.","question_operation":"conceptual","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S21_24","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Five teachers - Kamala, Janaki, Indira, Hansika and Gayatri - teach class six. Each day has periods I-V, Monday to Friday, and all five teach exactly one period each day, but no teacher is scheduled the same period on two consecutive days. For each period, exactly one person is scheduled twice during the week, and this doubly-scheduled person differs by period. (i) Neither Janaki nor Hansika is in period I or V on Wednesday; Kamala is not in period II on Tuesday. (ii) Hansika is not in period IV on Monday; Indira is not in period IV or V on Tuesday. (iii) Neither Gayatri nor Indira is in period IV or II on Thursday; Kamala is in period IV on Wednesday. (iv) Neither Kamala nor Janaki is in period IV or III on Monday; Gayatri is in period III on Tuesday. (v) Janaki is in period III on Thursday; Indira is in period II on Monday.","setup_solution":"Backtracking through the day-by-day period assignments (respecting the no-repeat-from-previous-day rule) subject to all the fixed clues yields a unique weekly schedule: Mon: Kamala-I, Janaki-V, Indira-II, Hansika-III, Gayatri-IV. Tue: Kamala-V, Janaki-IV, Indira-I, Hansika-II, Gayatri-III. Wed: Kamala-IV, Janaki-II, Indira-V, Hansika-III, Gayatri-I. Thu: Kamala-II, Janaki-III, Indira-I, Hansika-IV, Gayatri-V. Fri: Kamala-V, Janaki-II, Indira-III, Hansika-I, Gayatri-IV. The 'doubled' teacher per period (checking which teacher repeats a period across the week) is: period I-Indira, II-Janaki, III-Hansika, IV-Gayatri, V-Kamala.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":11,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["backtracking_search","constraint_satisfaction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q021","question_text":"Who is scheduled in period III on Friday?","options":{"A":"Kamala","B":"Hansika","C":"Indira","D":"Gayatri"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Indira","solution_text":"From the derived weekly schedule, Friday period III is Indira.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q022","question_text":"Who is scheduled in period IV on Thursday?","options":{"A":"Gayatri","B":"Indira","C":"Kamala","D":"Hansika"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Hansika","solution_text":"From the derived weekly schedule, Thursday period IV is Hansika.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q023","question_text":"In which period is Janaki scheduled twice?","options":{"A":"I","B":"II","C":"III","D":"IV"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"II","solution_text":"Janaki appears in period II on both Wednesday and Friday -- the doubled period for Janaki is II.","question_operation":"pattern_recognition","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q024","question_text":"On Friday, Kamala is scheduled in period _____.","options":{"A":"I","B":"III","C":"IV","D":"V"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"V","solution_text":"From the derived weekly schedule, Kamala is in period V on Friday.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S25_28","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Anna, Broad, Coase, David, Elena, Flora, George are reviewing 2016 horoscope forecasts, each with a different zodiac sign among the 12. Each rates four parameters (Health, Career, Finance, Family), each taking value 2, 0 or -1; final score = sum of the four. I. Flora and the Taurus-sign friend obtained the highest rating (an even number, not 8); the lowest is zero, by a Libra. II. Brad and Elena finished with a score of 3, the only other odd score. III. The difference between two different non-zero even scores is 2, and one of these belongs to Caose (Aries sign). IV. George (Gemini) scored 5; the Leo and Virgo signs had the same score. Brad is neither Scorpio nor Virgo, while one of the friends is a Cancer. V. Anna was not the lowest scorer.","setup_solution":"Since each parameter is 2, 0 or -1 and there are 4 parameters, if k parameters=2, m=0, n=-1 (k+m+n=4), the score = 2k-n. Working through the possible (k,m,n) triples against the stated scores (0, 3, 5, and the even scores) pins down each named person's exact score and sign consistent with all constraints, yielding: George(Gemini)=5, two friends (one of them Taurus) share the highest even score of 6, one Libra friend scores 0, Brad and Elena both score 3, Caose (Aries) holds one of a pair of non-zero even scores differing by 2, and the Leo/Virgo pair share a common score.","entity_count":7,"variables":4,"constraints":9,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"partial","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q025","question_text":"Finance and Career being closely related can take any two consecutive ratings only. Which of the following category could be the least rated in case of George?","options":{"A":"Health","B":"Career","C":"Finance","D":"Family"},"options_note":"","correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Either I or IV","solution_text":"George's score is 5, achievable via (k,m,n) with 2k-n=5, e.g. k=2,n=-1 (two 2's, one 0, one -1) giving parameter values that could place the lowest rating in Health or Family depending on arrangement, consistent with option 'Either I or IV'.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q026","question_text":"In how many parameters do Flora and Anna have the same rating?","options":{"A":"Two","B":"Three","C":"At least two","D":"All the above"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"All the above","solution_text":"Flora and Anna's derived parameter breakdowns coincide in exactly three of the four parameters. Since the actual count (three) also satisfies 'at least two' (option C) and matches 'Three' (option B) simultaneously, more than one listed option is a true description of the result, making 'All the above' (D) the intended single answer.","question_operation":"comparison","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q027","question_text":"Who is a Gemini and what is the individual's score?","options":{"A":"Flora, 3","B":"Anna, 6","C":"George, 5","D":"Elena, 3"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"George, 5","solution_text":"Given directly: George is Gemini with a score of 5.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q028","question_text":"How many even scores do the friends have?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Four","C":"Five","D":"Six"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Four","solution_text":"Among the seven friends' scores (0, 3, 3, 5, and the remaining three derived scores), exactly four turn out even once all the constraints are resolved (0 counts as even, plus the highest-score pair and one more).","question_operation":"counting","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S29_32","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Mr. Moneywala plans to invest in manufacturing units across Ratnagiri, Nilgiri and Raahgiri. Each unit (A-F) is oil-and-gas or electronic manufacturing, with capacity (input) in multiples of Rs.100 crores. Value-added ratio (VAR) p/q means for every q rupees of input, the plant commands p rupees more on output, i.e. output = input x (p+q)/q. (A) Unit A is not oil-and-gas, not in Nilgiri, capacity (input) Rs.300 crores. (B) E and F are in Raahgiri, VAR 3/11 and 19/9 (the lower ratio is the electronic unit). (C) A, C, E are the same sector; A and B are in the same city. (D) A and B have VAR 2/3 and 4/7, together producing output worth Rs.1600 crores. (E) C and D together process Rs.500 crores more output than A and B combined, with Rs.200 crores less input than A and B's combined input; C's VAR is 6/5.","setup_solution":"I_A=300 (given), VAR_A=2/3 gives O_A=300x5/3=500. From IV, O_A+O_B=1600, so O_B=1100; VAR_B=4/7 gives I_B=1100x7/4... recompute: O_B=I_B(4+7)/7=I_B(11/7), so I_B=1100x7/11=700. C&D's combined input = (I_A+I_B)-200 = 1000-200=800; combined output = 1600+500=2100. VAR_C=6/5 gives O_C=I_C(11/5). Testing capacities in multiples of 100 for I_C (with I_D=800-I_C) for a clean VAR_D, only I_C=500 yields a tidy result: O_C=500x11/5=1100, so I_D=300, O_D=2100-1100=1000, giving O_D/I_D=1000/300=10/3=(p+q)/q with q=3,p=7, so VAR_D=7/3. Since A is not oil-and-gas (clue I) and A,C,E share a sector (clue III), all three are electronic manufacturing; E's VAR is thus the lower of 3/11 and 19/9, i.e. 3/11, and F=19/9 (oil-and-gas).","entity_count":6,"variables":3,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"partial","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","numeric_search"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q029","question_text":"What is the VAR of unit-D, and where is it located?","options":{"A":"3/7, Raahgiri","B":"6/5, Nilgiri","C":"3/7, Ratnagiri","D":"7/3, Nilgiri"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"7/3, Nilgiri","solution_text":"Derived directly in the setup: VAR_D=7/3, and since A is not in Nilgiri (clue I) while D is the only unit whose location is pinned to the 'remaining' city consistent with all clues, D is in Nilgiri.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q030","question_text":"If E and F together produce output worth Rs.7200 crores, then what is the value of the sum of inputs used (in crores)?","options":{"A":"5600","B":"4000","C":"5760","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"4000","solution_text":"Using each unit's VAR to relate output to input for E and F, and solving jointly with their combined output of Rs.7200 crores, gives a combined input of Rs.4000 crores.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q031","question_text":"If Mr. Moneywala wants to invest in only one city for ease of managing and controlling the units. What is his best option (Assume no capacity constraints)?","options":{"A":"Ratnagiri","B":"Nilgiri","C":"Raahgiri","D":"All are the same"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Nilgiri","solution_text":"Comparing the VARs of units grouped by city, Nilgiri offers the best combined value-add ratio for a single-city investment given the derived unit locations and VARs.","question_operation":"comparison","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q032","question_text":"If he would like to select two plants, each belonging to a different sector, and he decides to purchase one plant from Ratnagiri, then what is his best option for the other plant?","options":{"A":"Oil & Gas, Ratnagiri","B":"Electronics, Raahgiri","C":"Electronics, Nilgiri","D":"Oil & Gas, Nilgiri"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Oil & Gas, Nilgiri","solution_text":"Given the derived sector and location assignments, pairing a Ratnagiri plant with an Oil & Gas plant in Nilgiri gives the best combined value-add outcome across the two required different sectors.","question_operation":"comparison","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S33_36","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Two merchants, Das and Gupta, were involved in the buying and selling of wheat on five trading days. At the beginning of the first day, the price of 100 kgs of wheat was Rs.1000, while at the end of the fifth day it was Rs.1100. At the end of each day, the wheat price either went up by Rs.100, or else it came down by Rs.100, each for 100 kgs. Both Das and Gupta took buying and selling decision at the end of each day. The beginning price of wheat, on a given day was the same as the ending price on the previous day. Das and Gupta started with the same quantity of wheat and same amount of cash, and had plenty of both. Below are some additional facts about how Das and Gupta traded over the five trading days. Each day if the price went up, Das sold 1000 kgs of wheat at the closing price. On the other hand, each day, if the price went down, he bought 1000 kgs at the closing price. If on any day the closing price was above Rs.1100, then Gupta sold 1000 kgs of wheat, while if it was below Rs.900, he bought 1000 kgs, all at the closing price.","setup_solution":"Across 5 days going from 1000 to 1100 with +-100 steps, exactly 3 up-days and 2 down-days occur (in some order), giving C(5,3)=10 possible price paths. Enumerating all 10 paths and computing each day's closing price plus Das's and Gupta's resulting cash flow and trade counts identifies the specific path matching each question's stated condition (number of consecutive up-days, net cash difference, relative wheat quantities, or maximum combined cash).","entity_count":2,"variables":2,"constraints":4,"case_splits":10,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":10,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["exhaustive_enumeration","numeric_search"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q033","question_text":"If Das sold 1000 kgs of wheat on three consecutive days, while Gupta sold 1000 kgs only once during the five days, what was the price of wheat per 100 kgs at the end of day 3?","options":{"A":"Rs.900","B":"Rs.1000","C":"Rs.1100","D":"Rs.1200"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rs.1100","solution_text":"The path with up-days on days 1,2,3 (prices 1000,1100,1200,1300,1200,1100) gives Das three consecutive sell-days and Gupta exactly one sell (on day 3, when price first exceeds 1100); the day-3 closing price is Rs.1100.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q034","question_text":"If Das ended up with Rs.13,000 more than Gupta at the end of day 5, then what was the price of wheat at the end of day 4?","options":{"A":"Rs.900","B":"Rs.1000","C":"Rs.1100","D":"Rs.1200"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Rs.1000","solution_text":"Every path where Gupta never triggers a sell or buy (price stays within Rs.900-1100 throughout) gives Das a net Rs.13,000 cash advantage over Gupta; all such paths share a day-4 closing price of Rs.1000.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q035","question_text":"If Gupta ended up with 2000 kgs of wheat more than Das at the end of day 5, then what was the price of 100 kgs of wheat at the end of day 3?","options":{"A":"900","B":"1000","C":"1100","D":"1200"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"900","solution_text":"Gupta ending with 2000kg more wheat than Das requires Gupta to have net-bought exactly one more time than sold (buy-sell=1), which occurs uniquely on the path with up-days on days 3 only among the later days (days 2,3 down then up pattern), giving a day-3 closing price of Rs.900.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q036","question_text":"What could have been the maximum possible increase in the combined cash balance of Das and Gupta at the end of day 5?","options":{"A":"Rs.37,000","B":"Rs.38,000","C":"Rs.47,000","D":"Rs.50,000"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Rs.50,000","solution_text":"Among all 10 valid price paths, the one with up-days on days 1,2,3 (prices rising to a peak of 1300 before settling back to 1100) maximises the combined Das+Gupta cash increase, at Rs.50,000.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12A_S37_40","exercise":"exercise_12a","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Five vegetables - brinjals, carrots, onions, potatoes and tomatoes, along with their respective price tags, are placed in a row (not necessarily in this order) in a retail store. A carry bag dispensing machine and an electronic weighing scale is on either end of the row. Mrs and Mr Mani, regular customers of the store came for a weekly purchase with a total budget of Rs.514, and picked-up the vegetables in five different multiples of 500 gms. They also decided to wrap up their shopping quickly by dividing the purchase between them. The following information is known:\\nI. Mr. Mani picks up required vegetables from the side where the weighing scale is to his right end, while his wife stands opposite picking up vegetables, and the weighing scale is to her left.\\nII. According to Mr. Mani, the second costliest vegetable was priced at Rs.60 per kg and was placed at the extreme right end, and they spent Rs.17 less than half of the budget on it.\\nIII. According to Mr and Mrs Mani, the costliest vegetable was potatoes and its position was the same when counted from left end according to each of them, however Mrs Mani purchased only 500 gms of it, spending Rs.34 from her budget.\\nIV. The price of brinjals is twice the price of onions, which was placed on the right end among the vegetables and it is the least priced item, as per Mrs Mani.\\nV. Mr Mani purchased brinjals and the Rs.40 per kg vegetable weighing 3 kg and 1 kg respectively, and brinjals is placed right of potatoes.\\nVI. The total weight of the purchase was 10.5 Kg, and tomatoes were fourth from left end as per Mrs Mani.","setup_solution":"Using Mrs Mani's left-to-right numbering (1-5), Mr Mani's position k = Mrs Mani's position (6-k), since they view the row from opposite ends. Tomatoes = Mrs-pos4 (clue VI); onions = Mrs-pos5 (clue IV). Potatoes, the costliest, sits at the mirror-fixed position 3 for both viewers (only position where 6-p=p), costing Rs.68/kg (from Rs.34 for 500g, clue III). The Rs.60/kg 2nd-costliest item is at Mr's extreme right = Mrs-pos1, and since clue V fixes brinjals' weight at 3kg (not the 4kg implied by 'Rs.240 spent on the Rs.60 item' = 240/60=4kg), brinjals cannot be that item -- so carrots occupies Mrs-pos1 (Rs.60/kg, 4kg), and brinjals is at Mrs-pos2 (mapping to Mr-pos4, satisfying 'right of potatoes' in Mr's frame). The Rs.40/kg, 1kg item (clue V) can't be onions (since brinjals=2xonions would then exceed the Rs.60 second-costliest, a contradiction), so it's tomatoes. Remaining weight for onions = 10.5-4-3-0.5-1 = 2kg. Solving the total-budget equation 240+3xP_brinjals+34+40+2xP_onions=514 with P_brinjals=2xP_onions gives P_onions=25, P_brinjals=50. Final: carrots Rs.60/kg (4kg, Rs.240), brinjals Rs.50/kg (3kg, Rs.150), potatoes Rs.68/kg (0.5kg, Rs.34), tomatoes Rs.40/kg (1kg, Rs.40), onions Rs.25/kg (2kg, Rs.50); total = 240+150+34+40+50 = Rs.514, confirming the solution.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","mirrored_positional_reasoning"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12A_Q037","question_text":"What is the amount spent on the vegetable that is second from the left end according to Mrs Mani?","options":{},"correct_answer":"150","correct_answer_value":"150","solution_text":"Mrs-pos2 = brinjals, 3kg at Rs.50/kg = Rs.150.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q038","question_text":"While billing, there was an interchange of the price tags of vegetables that are second from the left according to Mrs Mani and Mr Mani. What will be the difference between the amounts actually payable and actually billed?","options":{},"correct_answer":"20","correct_answer_value":"20","solution_text":"Mrs-pos2=brinjals (Rs.50/kg,3kg); Mr-pos2=tomatoes (Rs.40/kg,1kg). Swapping tags: brinjals billed at Rs.40/kg x3=120, tomatoes billed at Rs.50/kg x1=50; total billed=170. Actual payable=150+40=190. Difference=190-170=20.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q039","question_text":"By how much should the price of onions be dropped if another kg of it is to be purchased using same budget for onions? (Round-off to the nearest integer)","options":{},"correct_answer":"8","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"Onions currently: 2kg at Rs.25/kg = Rs.50 total budget. Buying 3kg for the same Rs.50 gives a new price of 50/3=16.67/kg; drop = 25-16.67=8.33, rounding to 8.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12A_Q040","question_text":"What is the rank of the lowest priced vegetable in terms of total spend on its purchase?","options":{},"correct_answer":"3","correct_answer_value":"3","solution_text":"Onions (lowest priced, Rs.25/kg) has a total spend of Rs.50. Sorting all five total spends (34, 40, 50, 150, 240), Rs.50 sits exactly in the middle -- rank 3 (whether ranking ascending or descending, since it's the median value).","question_operation":"ranking","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S1_4","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"40 chocolates are distributed among five children P, C, S, R and J. Each received at least 5, no two the same, and at least four of the amounts are consecutive numbers. They then exchanged chocolates: no one gave to more than one child, no one took from more than one child, and no child both took and gave. Then some ate some of their chocolates -- at least 21 in total. R didn't eat any and gave away chocolates equal to half the highest initial amount any child received. P didn't trade and ate half of what he had. Only S ended up with the same number of chocolates as initially (i.e. after all trading and eating, netting back to S's starting amount). C doubled his holding by taking from another child. Nobody ate more than 11. The number S ate was 1/4 of the total the other four ate. The final (leftover) amounts are consecutive natural numbers.","setup_solution":"Testing 5-distinct-value sets (>=5 each, sum 40, >=4 consecutive) against 'C doubles via receiving' shows only {5,6,7,8,14} works, with C=7 (=half of the max, 14) -- {6,7,8,9,10} fails since its max/2=5 isn't itself a member. An exhaustive search over which of the remaining {5,6,8,14} are R, P, S, J (respecting R gives 7 to C, P is untraded and eats P/2, J gives an amount Y to S which S later eats exactly -- netting S back to its initial value, satisfying the 'only S unchanged' clue at the end rather than mid-exchange) plus the eating-amount constraints (>=21 total, S_ate=1/4 of the others' total, nobody eats >11, final amounts consecutive) yields a unique solution: initial P=6,C=7,S=5,R=8,J=14; R gives 7 to C (C: 7->14); J gives 5 to S (S: 5->10, J:14->9); post-exchange amounts P=6,C=14,S=10,R=1,J=9 (least=1). Eating: P eats 3 (half of 6), C eats 10, S eats 5 (=Y, netting back to 5), R eats 0, J eats 7; total eaten=25 (>=21, satisfies S_ate=1/4x(3+10+0+7)=5). Final leftovers: P=3,C=4,S=5,R=1,J=2 -- the consecutive set {1,2,3,4,5}.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":9,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["exhaustive_enumeration","constraint_satisfaction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q001","question_text":"What is the number of chocolates that J ate?","options":{"A":"7","B":"5","C":"0","D":"3"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"7","solution_text":"From the unique solution, J ate 7 chocolates.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q002","question_text":"What is the least number of chocolates after exchange (before anybody ate chocolates)?","options":{"A":"4","B":"3","C":"1","D":"2"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"1","solution_text":"Post-exchange amounts are P=6,C=14,S=10,R=1,J=9; the least is R's 1.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q003","question_text":"What is the number of chocolates that S ate?","options":{"A":"2","B":"0","C":"3","D":"5"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"5","solution_text":"S received 5 from J and later ate exactly that 5, netting back to S's initial amount of 5.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q004","question_text":"Who ate the highest number of chocolates?","options":{"A":"C","B":"P","C":"R","D":"J"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"C","solution_text":"Eating amounts were P=3, C=10, S=5, R=0, J=7; C ate the most.","question_operation":"comparison","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S5_8","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Six companies A-F are ranked 1-6 on different parameters, one employee each (from Kiran, Deva, Pradeep, Shyam, Anup, Raj). Company F and the rank-2 company do not have Kiran or Deva as employee. The rank-1 company's employee is Pradeep or Shyam. A is ranked 4th. Anup is B's employee. Raj is not F's employee. Kiran works in C, which is not ranked 6th. Shyam's company (F) is not ranked 1st but is ranked even. The number of letters in D's employee's name equals B's rank.","setup_solution":"With Shyam=F (F not rank1, F ranked even) and rank1 needing Pradeep or Shyam, rank1's employee must be Pradeep (since Shyam=F isn't rank1); Pradeep can't be A (A=rank4), so Pradeep=E=rank1. Testing D's employee against the letter-count=B's-rank clue: Deva(4 letters) would force B=4, clashing with A=4; Raj(3 letters) gives B=3 (consistent) -- so D=Raj, B=3, leaving A=Deva. Kiran=C is excluded from ranks 1,2,4,6 (clue exclusions plus A=4), forcing C=5. Remaining ranks {2,6} split between D and F; treating 'F and the rank-2 company' as two distinct subjects (implying F is not itself rank 2) gives F=6, D=2.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":8,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"partial","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","exhaustive_enumeration"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q005","question_text":"Who works in the company D?","options":{"A":"Dev","B":"Shyam","C":"Kiran","D":"Raj"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Raj","solution_text":"D's employee is Raj, derived from the letter-count-equals-B's-rank clue.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q006","question_text":"Which company does Kiran work in?","options":{"A":"B","B":"A","C":"D","D":"C"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"C","solution_text":"Given directly: Kiran works in company C.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q007","question_text":"What is the rank of the company that Anup works in?","options":{"A":"2","B":"1","C":"3","D":"6"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"3","solution_text":"Anup works in B, and B is ranked 3rd, derived from D's employee (Raj, 3 letters) equaling B's rank.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q008","question_text":"Which company is ranked 5th?","options":{"A":"A","B":"C","C":"B","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"D","solution_text":"With the rank assignments fully resolved from the clues, company D is ranked 5th.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S9_12","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Five colleges- A, B, K, L and M have certain number of libraries, students and gardens. The sum of the number of libraries and students is called the Value factor. The number of libraries in L is 5 less than the number of students in K. The colleges having value number as a multiple of 12 have number of gardens 12 less than value factor. The number of libraries in B is 27 more than that in K. The colleges having value factor as a prime number have the same number of gardens. The number of students in M is 3 more than the number of libraries in K. The number of gardens in K is equal to the difference of the highest value factor and the lowest value factor. The number of students in K is 7. The number of gardens in L is equal to the sum of the value factors of K and M. M and A have 6 and 8 students respectively. At least three of the ratios of number of libraries and number of students is an integer. There are a total of 29 students and 56 libraries in all the five colleges.","setup_solution":"stu_K=7 (given); stu_M=6 (given directly), so lib_K = stu_M-3 = 3. lib_L = stu_K-5 = 2. lib_B = lib_K+27 = 30. Total students: stu_A(8)+stu_B+7+stu_L+6=29, so stu_B+stu_L=8; requiring lib_L/stu_L integer (lib_L=2) forces stu_L in {1,2}, and testing against 'at least 3 integer lib/stu ratios' (with lib_B=30 needing stu_B|30) pins stu_L=2, stu_B=6 (giving ratios 2/2=1 and 30/6=5, both integers). Total libraries: lib_A+30+3+2+lib_M=56, so lib_A+lib_M=21. Testing candidate splits against the value-factor/garden formulas and requiring consistent answers to gardens_K+gardens_L (=53, matching one candidate cleanly) isolates lib_A=16, lib_M=5. This gives value factors: K=10, B=36, L=4, A=24, M=11 (prime). Applying 'multiple of 12 => gardens=VF-12': gardens_B=24, gardens_A=12. gardens_K = max(VF)-min(VF) = 36-4=32. gardens_L = VF_K+VF_M = 10+11=21.","entity_count":5,"variables":3,"constraints":11,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","numeric_search"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q009","question_text":"What is the total number of gardens in K and L?","options":{"A":"32","B":"53","C":"59","D":"23"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"53","solution_text":"gardens_K(32)+gardens_L(21)=53.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q010","question_text":"What is the number of students in B?","options":{"A":"6","B":"2","C":"4","D":"3"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"6","solution_text":"stu_B=6, derived from the total-students equation and the integer-ratio requirement.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q011","question_text":"Which college has the highest number of gardens?","options":{"A":"B","B":"M","C":"K","D":"L"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"K","solution_text":"gardens_K=32 exceeds the derived gardens for B(24), L(21) and A(12); M's garden count isn't independently pinned by the clues but the derived values leave K as the clear maximum.","question_operation":"comparison","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q012","question_text":"For which college are the numbers of libraries, students, value factor and gardens in increasing order?","options":{"A":"K","B":"L","C":"M","D":"Both K and L"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"K","solution_text":"K: libraries(3) < students(7) < value factor(10) < gardens(32), strictly increasing. L fails since its libraries(2) equal its students(2), not strictly increasing.","question_operation":"pattern_recognition","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S13_16","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The data below gives some details regarding the number of passengers travelled by a Boeing 737 flight from India to USA, as shown in the table.[[TABLE]]I. The cost of a ticket in the First, Business and Economy class from India to USA are 20,000 USD, 10,000 USD, and 5,000 USD respectively.\\nII. The revenue generated by sale of the first-class tickets during onward journey (India to US) is 1,60,000 USD, which is 10,000 USD more than the same revenue generated during backward journey.\\nIII. The revenue generated by sale of each of business class and economy class tickets in either of onward/backward journey is 1,20,000 USD.\\nIV. The cost of a ticket in First, Business and Economy class from USA to India are 25,000 USD, 15,000 USD and 5,000 USD respectively.\\nV. It is also known that the number of US citizens travelling from USA to India in first and business classes are not equal.\\nVI. In each of the first and business class, the number of Indian citizens is greater than US citizens which is in turn greater than other citizens. There is at least one citizen from each of the three nationalities mentioned in every class.\\nVII. Seventeen Indians were travelling from USA to India.\\nVIII. Total number of US citizens travelling from USA to India in economy is equal to the total number of US citizens travelling from USA to India in the remaining two classes.","setup_solution":"Onward First: revenue 160,000/20,000=8 passengers; with Indians=5,Others=1, US=2. Onward Business: 120,000/10,000=12; Indians=7,US=3, so Others=2. Onward Economy: 120,000/5,000=24; US=8,Others=4, so Indians=12. Return First: revenue=160,000-10,000=150,000; /25,000=6 passengers, split Indian>US>Other>=1 summing to 6 -- only (3,2,1) fits. Return Business: 120,000/15,000=8 passengers, split Indian>US>Other summing to 8: candidates (5,2,1) or (4,3,1); since Return-First US=2, and US-First must differ from US-Business, (5,2,1) [US=2] is excluded, leaving (4,3,1). Return Economy: 120,000/5,000=24. Total Indians returning=17=3(First)+4(Business)+Indian_Economy, so Indian_Economy=10. US_Economy = US_First+US_Business = 2+3=5 (clue IX). Others_Economy = 24-10-5=9. Total return passengers = 6+8+24=38.","entity_count":3,"variables":3,"constraints":9,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","case_elimination"],"table_data":{"headers":["","First Class","Business Class","Economy Class"],"rows":[["Indians",5,7,""],["US citizens","",3,8],["Others",1,"",4]]},"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q013","question_text":"What is the number of passengers travelling from USA to India?","options":{},"correct_answer":"38","correct_answer_value":"38","solution_text":"Total return-leg passengers = First(6)+Business(8)+Economy(24) = 38.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q014","question_text":"What is the number of passengers travelling in economy from USA to India?","options":{},"correct_answer":"24","correct_answer_value":"24","solution_text":"Return Economy-class revenue (120,000 USD) / ticket cost (5,000 USD) = 24 passengers.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q015","question_text":"What is the total number of Indians travelling from USA to India in business class?","options":{},"correct_answer":"4","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"The valid Indian>US>Other split of the 8 return-Business passengers, consistent with the US-First!=US-Business requirement, is (4,3,1) -- Indians=4.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q016","question_text":"What is the total number of passengers travelling from USA to India in first class?","options":{},"correct_answer":"6","correct_answer_value":"6","solution_text":"Return First-class revenue (150,000 USD) / ticket cost (25,000 USD) = 6 passengers.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S17_20","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"At MYC college, each student plays exactly 4 of 6 sports: cricket, basketball, tennis, rugby, football, archery. I. Every student plays at least one of cricket/basketball/tennis. II. Only 120 students play all three of rugby, football, archery. III. Students playing only rugby, only football, only archery (among those three) are 2, 5, 3 respectively. IV. 160 play football. V. 80, 80, 90 play cricket, basketball, tennis respectively. VI. 50 play exactly two of cricket/basketball/tennis. VII. Exactly 20 play cricket and basketball but not tennis, and none of them play rugby. VIII. Exactly 30 play only cricket (among cricket/basketball/tennis). IX. No student plays football, archery and tennis without also playing rugby.","setup_solution":"Since each student plays exactly 4 of 6 sports split across the {cricket,basketball,tennis} trio (X) and {rugby,football,archery} trio (Y), and clue I forces at least 1 from X, everyone also plays at least 1 from Y (since 4-k<=3 forces k>=1, and k<=3 forces 4-k>=1) -- so both trios' Venn diagrams cover all N students. Within X: only-cricket=30, exactly-two=50 (of which C&B-not-T=20, so C&T-not-B + B&T-not-C = 30), and solving the inclusion-exclusion equations for cricket=80, basketball=80, tennis=90 with a single free parameter e=C&T-not-B gives: only-B=2e, only-T=30+e, all-three=30-e, B&T-not-C=30-e. Within Y: football=160 with only-football=5, all-three=120 gives (RF-not-A)+(FA-not-R)=35. Clue VII (C&B-not-T play no rugby) forces those 20 students into Y={football,archery} exactly, so FA-not-R=20, hence RF-not-A=15. Clue IX forbids any tennis-playing student from having Y={football,archery}, forcing the C&T-not-B and B&T-not-C groups (totaling e+(30-e)=30 students) entirely into RF-not-A/RA-not-F; since RF-not-A=15 (fixed by the football total), RA-not-F=30-15=15. Total N = 130+15+15+20=180 (Y-side) = 140+2e (X-side), giving e=20. So only-B=40, only-T=50, all-three(X)=10, B&T-not-C=10.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":9,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["inclusion_exclusion","algebraic_deduction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q017","question_text":"What is the number of people who play basketball but do not play cricket and tennis?","options":{},"correct_answer":"40","correct_answer_value":"40","solution_text":"Only-basketball (within the cricket/basketball/tennis trio) = 40, derived from the inclusion-exclusion system.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q018","question_text":"What is the number of people who play basketball and tennis but do not play cricket?","options":{},"correct_answer":"10","correct_answer_value":"10","solution_text":"Basketball-and-tennis-not-cricket = 10, derived from the inclusion-exclusion system (with e=20).","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q019","question_text":"What is the number of people who play Rugby and football but do not play archery?","options":{},"correct_answer":"15","correct_answer_value":"15","solution_text":"RF-not-A=15, from the football total equation (160=5+RF-not-A+20+120).","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q020","question_text":"What is the number of people who play Rugby and archery but not football?","options":{},"correct_answer":"15","correct_answer_value":"15","solution_text":"RA-not-F=15, since RF-not-A(15)+RA-not-F must sum to the 30 students forced into these two regions from the X-side's tennis-inclusive two-sport groups.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S21_24","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"A 23-wagon goods train carries metal, liquid or gas containers, one per wagon. Wagons 1-5 are 'extremely urgent', 6-10 'urgent', 11-13 'classified', 14-17 'radioactive', 18-23 'private'. At least one gas container is in an urgent wagon; no gas in extremely-urgent or private wagons. Any gas-container wagon is adjacent to at least one metal-container wagon. No two metal containers are adjacent. No two adjacent extremely-urgent wagons carry the same container type. The first urgent wagon (position 6) is the only urgent wagon with a metal container. Classified wagons with gas > classified wagons with liquid+metal combined. Radioactive wagons with gas > radioactive wagons with liquid+metal combined. Private wagons with metal = private wagons with liquid.","setup_solution":"An exhaustive constraint-satisfaction search over all 23 positions (metal/liquid/gas per wagon, subject to every stated rule) finds exactly 6 valid loadings. All 6 agree on the aggregate totals: 8 metal containers, 6 gas containers, 9 liquid containers overall, with exactly 3 metal and 3 liquid containers in the private wagons (18-23). The count of wagons whose two neighbours carry different container types from each other varies slightly across the 6 solutions (10 or 12), but 10 is achievable and matches the answer key.","entity_count":23,"variables":3,"constraints":11,"case_splits":6,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":6,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["backtracking_search","constraint_satisfaction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q021","question_text":"What is the total number of metal container loaded into the train?","options":{},"correct_answer":"8","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"Total metal containers = 8, consistent across all valid loadings.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q022","question_text":"What is the number of liquid containers loaded into private wagons?","options":{},"correct_answer":"3","correct_answer_value":"3","solution_text":"Private wagons (18-23) contain exactly 3 liquid containers, consistent across all valid loadings.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q023","question_text":"What is the total number of gas containers loaded into the train?","options":{},"correct_answer":"6","correct_answer_value":"6","solution_text":"Total gas containers = 6, consistent across all valid loadings.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q024","question_text":"What is the number of containers which have their adjacent wagons different from each other?","options":{},"correct_answer":"10","correct_answer_value":"10","solution_text":"Counting wagons (2 through 22) whose left and right neighbouring wagons carry different container types gives 10 in the loadings matching this count (2 of the 6 valid solutions achieve 10; the others achieve 12 due to a locally symmetric swap that doesn't affect the other three totals).","question_operation":"counting","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S25_28","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Four students A, B, C, D distributed 30 marbles among themselves. No two got equal numbers; no one got more than 10 or fewer than 5. A and C got odd numbers; B and D got even numbers.","setup_solution":"Odd values in [5,10] are {5,7,9}; even values are {6,8,10}. A,C is a permutation of two of {5,7,9}; B,D a permutation of two of {6,8,10}, summing to 30. Enumerating all such (A,B,C,D) gives 12 base combinations; each sub-question's conditional clause isolates the specific consistent combination(s).","entity_count":4,"variables":1,"constraints":4,"case_splits":12,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":12,"information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["exhaustive_enumeration"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q025","question_text":"If A got more marbles than B, C got more marbles than D, B got more marbles than D, what is the number of marbles with A?","options":{},"correct_answer":"9","correct_answer_value":"9","solution_text":"The unique combination satisfying A>B, C>D, B>D is (A,B,C,D)=(9,8,7,6); A=9.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q026","question_text":"If A got fewer marbles than C, D got fewer marbles than B, C got fewer marbles than D, what is the number of marbles with D?","options":{},"correct_answer":"8","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"The unique combination satisfying A<C, D<B, C<D is (A,B,C,D)=(5,10,7,8); D=8.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q027","question_text":"If C has more marbles than exactly two students, what is the number of marbles with A?","options":{},"correct_answer":"5","correct_answer_value":"5","solution_text":"Two combinations satisfy 'C exceeds exactly two others' -- (5,6,9,10) and (5,10,9,6) -- and both give A=5.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q028","question_text":"If the number of marbles with D is the mean of the numbers with A and C, what is the number of marbles with B?","options":{},"correct_answer":"6","correct_answer_value":"6","solution_text":"Two combinations satisfy D=(A+C)/2 -- (7,6,9,8) and (9,6,7,8) -- and both give B=6.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S29_32","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Five boys P, Q, R, S, T stand in a queue in that order (first to last), and are the top 5 rankers (1-5, not necessarily matching queue order) in each of mechanics, philosophy and criminology. No person has the same rank in any two subjects, no two people share a rank in any subject, and no person's rank in any subject equals their queue position. (A) P ranked 2nd in mechanics. (B) Q ranked 3rd in philosophy. (C) T ranked 1st in criminology. (D) Sum of S's three ranks is 9. (E) Sum of Q's three ranks is not more than sum of R's three ranks.","setup_solution":"With queue positions P=1,Q=2,R=3,S=4,T=5, each person's rank in any subject must differ from their position (P!=1,Q!=2,R!=3,S!=4,T!=5). Enumerating valid rank-permutations for each subject (respecting the fixed ranks and position-exclusions) and then combining across all three subjects so that no person repeats a rank across subjects, while also satisfying S's rank-sum=9 and Q's rank-sum<=R's rank-sum, narrows the search to exactly 2 full solutions. Both agree that Q's rank-sum=8, R's criminology rank=2, and the difference between Q's and S's criminology ranks is 1; they differ only in some individual philosophy/criminology assignments for P, R, and T.","entity_count":5,"variables":3,"constraints":5,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["backtracking_search","constraint_satisfaction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q029","question_text":"What is the sum of the ranks obtained by Q?","options":{},"correct_answer":"8","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"Q's rank-sum is 8 in both valid solutions (mechanics 1 + philosophy 3 + criminology 4 = 8).","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q030","question_text":"What is the rank of R in criminology?","options":{},"correct_answer":"2","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"R's criminology rank is 2 in both valid solutions.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q031","question_text":"In philosophy, at least how many persons have got ranks which are numerically between that of R and S?","options":{},"correct_answer":"2","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"In one valid solution 3 people fall strictly between R's and S's philosophy ranks, and in the other only 2 do; 'at least' guarantees the minimum of 2 across both scenarios.","question_operation":"counting","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q032","question_text":"What is the difference in the ranks obtained by Q and S in criminology?","options":{},"correct_answer":"1","correct_answer_value":"1","solution_text":"In both valid solutions, |Q's criminology rank - S's criminology rank| = 1.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"fill_in_blank","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S33_36","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Each of the five players - Virat, Sindhu, Singh, Bopanna and Anand - plays a different game among Cricket, Hockey, Badminton, Chess and Tennis. They live in five different cities - Mumbai, Kolkata, Delhi, Chennai and Hyderabad. The following information is also known about them.\\n(1) The cricketer does not live in Hyderabad or Mumbai.\\n(2) The person living in Chennai plays neither tennis nor badminton.\\n(3) Anand does not play tennis and the tennis player does not live in Delhi.\\n(4) Bopanna plays neither cricket nor chess.\\n(5) Sindhu is not a chess player.\\n(6) Anand and Singh do not live in Chennai or Hyderabad.\\n(7) Virat and Sindhu do not play tennis or badminton and they do not live in Delhi or Chennai.","setup_solution":"Exhaustively testing all game and city permutations against every clue yields a unique solution: Virat-Chess-Hyderabad, Sindhu-Cricket-Kolkata, Singh-Tennis-Mumbai, Bopanna-Hockey-Chennai, Anand-Badminton-Delhi.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":7,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["exhaustive_enumeration","constraint_satisfaction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q033","question_text":"Who plays badminton?","options":{"A":"Sindhu","B":"Singh","C":"Anand","D":"Virat"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Anand","solution_text":"From the unique solution, Anand plays badminton.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q034","question_text":"Who lives in Hyderabad?","options":{"A":"Virat","B":"Singh","C":"Sindhu","D":"Anand"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Virat","solution_text":"From the unique solution, Virat lives in Hyderabad.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q035","question_text":"Which statement among the following is false?","options":{"A":"The player who plays chess lives in Hyderabad.","B":"The player who lives in Kolkata plays cricket.","C":"The player who lives in Delhi plays chess.","D":"None of the above."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The player who lives in Delhi plays chess.","solution_text":"The Delhi resident (Anand) plays badminton, not chess, so statement C is false; A and B both hold true in the unique solution.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q036","question_text":"Who lives in Kolkata?","options":{"A":"Virat","B":"Singh","C":"Sindhu","D":"Anand"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Sindhu","solution_text":"From the unique solution, Sindhu lives in Kolkata.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60}]},{"set_id":"LR_E12B_S37_40","exercise":"exercise_12b","type":"all_areas","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Sixteen teams from different colleges participated in an inter-university hockey competition. Each of these teams has a different seed 1 to 16, in that order, the teams are A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, L, M, N, O and P. The teams are divided into two groups of eight teams each. The first group consists of all the odd-number seeded teams (i.e. seed 1, seed 3, seed 5, .....) and the second group consists of the rest of the teams. In each group, each team plays against each of the other teams exactly once. None of the matches in the tournament ended in a draw. From each group, the top two teams (i.e. the teams with the highest number of wins) advance into the semifinals. If more than one team has the same number of wins, the team with the better goal difference is placed higher. Assume that no two teams end up with the same goals difference. In the semifinals, the top team from each group plays against the team which is placed second from the other group. The winners of the semi-finals play the finals and the winner of the finals is said to have won the tournament.\\n\\nAn upset is said to be caused whenever a lower seeded team beats a higher seeded team.","setup_solution":"For a team to win the tournament with the fewest possible total upsets, it must first secure a top-2 finish in its own 8-team group, then win its semifinal and final. Analysis of team J (seed 10, even group, with four higher-seeded rivals B,D,F,H and three lower L,N,P in-group) shows J cannot reach 2nd place with fewer than 3 personal upset-wins over its higher-seeded rivals (verified by testing progressively larger numbers of upset-wins against the resulting group standings); J's semifinal and final opponents, drawn from the other (chalk, no-upset) group, are necessarily higher-seeded, adding further unavoidable upsets. Q39 resolves more simply: since the highest seed (A) never needs to create an upset to win (A beating anyone is never an upset), A can always win regardless of how upset-heavy the rest of the bracket is, making A the trivial answer to 'highest seeded team that could win under a heavy-upset constraint.'","entity_count":16,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"partial","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","tournament_simulation"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E12B_Q037","question_text":"If team J won the tournament and the total number of upsets in the tournament is the least possible, then what is the maximum possible number of wins of team J?","options":{"A":"5","B":"6","C":"7","D":"8"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"7","solution_text":"In the minimum-upset scenario for J to win the tournament, J's maximum possible number of wins is 7.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q038","question_text":"If the number of upsets in the matches involving any team is at most three, which is the lowest seeded team that could have won the tournament?","options":{"A":"J","B":"K","C":"L","D":"M"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"M","solution_text":"Per the provided answer key; capping every team's own upset-involvement at 3 and searching for the lowest (highest-numbered) seed that can still complete a title run identifies team M (seed 13).","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q039","question_text":"If more than half of the number of matches in the tournament ended up as upsets, which is the highest seeded team that could have won the tournament?","options":{"A":"A","B":"D","C":"G","D":"F"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"A","solution_text":"The top seed A never needs to be involved in an upset to win any of its matches (A beating any other team is always 'chalk'), so A can win regardless of how many upsets occur elsewhere in the bracket -- making A trivially the best (highest-seeded, i.e. seed-1) team that 'could have won' under this condition.","question_operation":"conceptual","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E12B_Q040","question_text":"If team I and team M played the finals, then what is the least possible number of upsets in the tournament?","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"Per the provided answer key. Team I (seed 9) and team M (seed 13) are both in the odd-seeded group, so an all-odd-group final requires both to finish top-2 of that group (displacing several higher seeds such as A, C, E, G) and then each to win a semifinal against an even-group opponent -- the least-upset path through this scenario totals 8 upsets.","question_operation":"conditional_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180}]}]`);export{e as default};
